# Target C: finite release of sparse packets anchored only at loaded vertices

Date: 2026-10-05. Base main: `ad8809fd96c2c0af9a6de4d965e9ab33efe092c6`.
State: `2026-10-05.audit.59`.
Classification: **proved candidate awaiting independent audit**; artifact
lifecycle **unreviewed**. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Exact chain

```
feasible equal-cardinality replacement removing an original contact
+ strict upper bounds at every inserted site
+ only loaded retained core anchors and sufficiently few new contacts
-> positive pinned stresses reduce to collinear chains in a thin strip
-> an explicit straight finite motion opens every new unit contact
-> fewer contacts at the same or smaller diameter
-> contradiction of canonical contact minimality.
```

This is a finite opening theorem with its nonlinear step proved, not an
assertion that a zero multiplier automatically integrates. It excludes
small static replacement attempts; it does not construct the larger
packet needed for closure. Retained source isolates are free variables
in the opening, not discarded labels or rigidity coordinates.

## 2. Finite replacement statement

Let A be a canonical triangle-free contact-minimal global minimizer of
diameter D, with positive-contact core V and actual loaded vertices B.
Canonical H=H_* gives every source isolate strict upper slack. Let T be
any common bound on the distances of all actual diameter midpoints from
one center, as supplied by canonical Section 58, FS.6.

Delete R subset A with at least one original contact incident to R.
Insert Y with |Y|=|R|, obtaining an actual unit-separated configuration
A'. Assume every pair involving an inserted point is strictly below D.
Triangle-freeness of A' is not required: the opening below removes all
new contacts. Let J be the number of unit edges of A' with at least one
endpoint in Y. Assume every retained original nonisolated vertex incident
to such an edge belongs to B, and

    J(J+2T)<D/4.                                      (2.1)

Then this replacement cannot exist for A.

The case J=0 already contradicts contact minimality. For J>=1, retain all
original nonisolated vertices outside R as fixed points. Treat Y and
**every retained original isolate** as free. Their initially active unit
edges are precisely the J new edges: an original isolate had no old
contact, and retained core-core contacts are fixed and excluded from this
opening system. All upper pairs involving free points are strict, as are
all initially inactive lower pairs. Cardinality is never changed again.

## 3. Positive-stress components are confined to thin strips

Let A_L be the matrix of active squared-distance differentials on the
free coordinates. Consider all nonnegative pinned equilibrium stresses

    lambda>=0, A_L^T lambda=0.

Choose one whose positive support E+ is the union of all possible
positive supports, by adding finitely many witnessing stresses and
normalizing. If no stress exists, E+ is empty.

For any connected positive-support component, equilibrium at each free
vertex makes its position a weighted average of its neighbors. A component
without a fixed pin is impossible: a uniquely extreme free point violates
positive equilibrium. With pins, the discrete harmonic maximum principle
puts all its free positions in the convex hull of its pins. Equivalently,
the grounded positive Laplacian is invertible and its harmonic extension
has nonnegative weights summing to one.

Every pin is an original actual loaded vertex. Any two pins of the
component are joined by a path of at most J unit edges, so their distance
is at most J. Choose one actual diameter partner for each pin v_i and set

    n_i=(v_i-a_i)/D, m_i=(v_i+a_i)/2.

Then D(n_i-n_j)=2(v_i-v_j)-2(m_i-m_j), and

    sin(theta_ij/2)<=(J+2T)/D=:rho.

Condition (2.1) gives rho<1/(4J)<=1/4. Thus all pin normals lie in one
ordinary angular interval of width at most alpha=2asin(rho)<pi/2.
To check the common interval, lift relative to the first normal into
[-alpha,alpha]; the extreme difference is less than pi, so the pairwise
bound forces that difference to be at most alpha.

Each chosen normal uniquely supports the old source hull at its pin,
because for every other old source point w,

    n_i dot(w-v_i)<=-|w-v_i|^2/(2D)<0.

Order the pin normals in the short interval. Chord directions between
pins lie between the corresponding quarter-turned support normals.
Using the first quarter-turn as the horizontal axis, the transverse
coordinate difference of any two pins is therefore at most

    J sin(alpha)<=2J rho<1/2.

The pins, and hence every free point in their harmonic convex hull, lie
in a strip of width w<1/2. One-pin components would collapse their free
points to the pin, contradicting unit separation, so cause no exception.

## 4. Strip separation forces straight chains

Fix a free point of a positive-support component. All its stressed unit
neighbors are in the same strip. For two unit neighbors on the same
horizontal side, their horizontal coordinate difference is at most
1-sqrt(1-w^2), and their vertical difference is at most w. Thus their
squared separation is at most

    (1-sqrt(1-w^2))^2+w^2
    =2-2sqrt(1-w^2)<1.

This contradicts source unit separation. There is at most one stressed
unit neighbor on each side, so every free stressed vertex has degree at
most two. Positive equilibrium excludes degree one. At degree two the
two unit vectors are opposite and the two weights equal.

Consequently the positive-support graph is a union of collinear chains
of free vertices between fixed pins. Split a component at its fixed pins;
pins can branch, but no free vertex belongs to two different chains.
A chain cannot close without pins because opposite unit vectors would
make a nontrivial straight closed polygon. At each chain vertex the
perpendicular velocity is an independent variable in ker A_L[E+].

## 5. Opening weak rows and buckling the positive chains

The elementary linear alternative supplies a velocity u_0 with

    A_L u_0>=0,
    (A_L u_0)_e=0 for e in E+,
    (A_L u_0)_e>0 for e outside E+.

Here is the precise support argument. For K={u:A_Lu>=0}, a row outside
E+ cannot vanish on every u in K. Otherwise -A_e belongs to the dual
cone generated by the rows of A_L, giving a nonnegative zero relation
with positive coefficient on e, contrary to its absence from E+.
Choose a nonnegative velocity making each such row positive and add
them. Every row in E+ must vanish on K by summing against its positive
stress coefficient. This proves the displayed alternative.

Perturb u_0 arbitrarily little within ker A_L[E+] while preserving all
the strict weak-row inequalities. On every collinear stressed chain,
free perpendicular velocities are independent. For every stressed edge,
the equality u_i=u_j is therefore a proper linear subspace of this
kernel, including an edge to a fixed pin whose velocity is zero.
Avoid the finite union of these proper subspaces. The resulting u has

    A_L[E+]u=0, u_i-u_j!=0 on every edge in E+,

and still strictly opens every other active row to first order.

Now use the actual straight motion p_i(t)=p_i+t*u_i at free vertices and
keep pins fixed. For each originally unit edge,

    |p_i(t)-p_j(t)|^2
    =1+t*(A_Lu)_e+t^2|u_i-u_j|^2.

Every weak row opens linearly. Every positive-support row opens by its
strictly positive quadratic term. Thus every new unit contact becomes
strictly longer than one for small positive t. All inactive lower and
strict upper constraints persist by their finite margins. No convergence
or active-set pivot claim is hidden in this step.

## 6. Canonical contradiction and scope

The endpoint retains cardinality n, unit separation and diameter at most
D. All contacts involving free points have disappeared; the only contacts
left are retained old core-core contacts. At least one original contact
was deleted by R, so the endpoint has fewer contacts than A. Its contact
graph is triangle-free even if A' initially was not, since it is an
induced subgraph of the original contact graph plus isolates.
If its diameter is below D, global optimality fails. Otherwise it is a
triangle-free global minimizer with fewer contacts. Contradiction.

Therefore any feasible equal-cardinality replacement of a contact-active
set with strict inserted upper bounds must either touch a retained
**unloaded core anchor**, or satisfy

    J(J+2T)>=D/4.

This statement allows tiny motions of retained original isolates during
the opening; their original lower and upper slack is essential and is
not assigned a uniform size. It does not permit moving the fixed core
pins, nor does it classify packets with new active diameter pairs.

In the primitive all-loaded shell V=B, the unloaded-anchor alternative
is absent. Any such packet needs J>=sqrt(T^2+D/4)-T. Since canonical
T=O(D^(3/4)), this is Omega(D^(1/4)). In particular a bounded contact-budget
exchange cannot close that branch. A proper consecutive loaded-hull run
of length ell removes ell+1 contacts in the primitive cycle; deleting
isolates adds no contacts. An equal-budget packet therefore requires
ell+1>=sqrt(T^2+D/4)-T. Static exchanges of a bounded number of guards
are excluded; a growing collective packet or moved core anchors remain.

This is a necessary-size theorem, not enough capacity, an asymptotic
closure, or a lower bound on the original stress coefficients. Both
exhaustive C-II branches remain open. Generic isolate avoidance is not
needed here because retained isolates are allowed to move with the packet.

## 7. Validation

The finite geometry, cone alternative and straight-motion identity are
analytic. No numerical verifier or global-optimality computation is
claimed. Required repository checks validate state and artifact
consistency; this new proof still requires independent mathematical audit.
